  "title": "Lowell mill women's turn-out of 1834",
  "summary": "In February 1834 about 800 women textile workers in Lowell, Massachusetts walked out after mill agents announced a citywide 12.5 percent wage cut. The turn-out was hurried and poorly organised after leader Julia Wilson was fired, and strikers either went back to work or left Lowell without winning their demand. A better-organised strike followed in 1836.",
